(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-12-03"] = {"3.1":{"s":"Ionic equilibrium is the balance between ions and un-ionised molecules in a solution. The equilibrium rules from Class 11 are applied here to acids, bases, salts, buffers and sparingly soluble salts."},"3.2":{"s":"Electrolytes give ions in water; non-electrolytes, such as sugar, stay as molecules. Strong electrolytes (NaCl, HCl, NaOH) ionise almost completely. Weak electrolytes (acetic acid, ammonium hydroxide) ionise only a little. The degree of dissociation α is the fraction that has ionised."},"3.3":{"s":"Three ways to define acids and bases. Arrhenius: acids give H⁺ in water and bases give OH⁻. Brønsted–Lowry: an acid donates a proton and a base accepts one; each acid has a conjugate base. Lewis: an acid accepts an electron pair and a base donates one.","w":["Water is amphoteric: an acid towards NH₃ and a base towards HCl.","Every Brønsted base is a Lewis base, but not every Lewis acid is a Brønsted acid (BF₃ has no proton)."]},"3.4":{"s":"Strong acids and bases ionise fully. A weak acid has a dissociation constant Ka; a larger Ka means a stronger acid. Ostwald's dilution law: for a weak electrolyte α = √(K/c), so it ionises more as the solution is diluted.","f":[{"x":"K_a = [H^(+)]*[A^(-)]/[HA]","n":"Dissociation constant of a weak acid HA; K_b for a weak base is written the same way with OH^(-).","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 3.4, p.49"},{"x":"K_a = alpha^2*c/(1 - alpha)","n":"Ostwald's dilution law; c = concentration in mol/L.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 3.4, p.50"},{"x":"alpha = sqrt(K_a/c)","n":"Weak acid with alpha much less than 1; for a weak base alpha = sqrt(K_b/c).","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 3.4, p.50"},{"x":"[H_3O^(+)] = alpha*c","n":"Hydronium ion concentration of a weak monobasic acid.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 3.4, p.51"}],"w":["Diluting a weak acid raises α but lowers [H⁺], because c falls faster than α rises."]},"3.5":{"s":"Water ionises very slightly into H⁺ and OH⁻. Their product, Kw, is 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ at 25 °C. pH = −log[H⁺] and pOH = −log[OH⁻], and pH + pOH = 14 at 25 °C. pH 7 is neutral, below 7 is acidic, and above 7 is basic.","f":[{"x":"K_w = [H_3O^(+)]*[OH^(-)] = 1.0*10^-14","n":"Ionic product of water at 298 K.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 3.5, p.51"}],"w":["The ionic product of water rises with temperature, so neutral water has pH below 7 when hot; it is still neutral."]},"3.6":{"s":"pH tells how acidic a solution is: it is the negative logarithm, to base 10, of the hydronium ion concentration in mol/dm³. pOH is the same quantity for hydroxide ions. In water at 298 K the two add up to 14. A solution with pH 7 is neutral, below 7 acidic, above 7 basic. Each step of one pH unit is a tenfold change in the hydronium ion concentration.","f":[{"x":"pH = -log_10([H_3O^(+)])","n":"Concentration in mol/dm^3. Neutral water at 298 K has [H_3O^(+)] = 1.0 × 10^-7, so pH = 7.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 3.6, p.52"},{"x":"pOH = -log_10([OH^(-)])","n":"The same for hydroxide ions.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 3.6, p.52"},{"x":"pH + pOH = 14","n":"Valid for aqueous solutions at 298 K (K_w = 1.0 × 10^-14).","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 3.6, p.52"}],"w":["pH 3 has ten times more hydronium ions than pH 4.","pH + pOH = 14 holds only at 298 K; at other temperatures the ionic product of water differs."]},"3.7":{"s":"Hydrolysis: the ions of some salts react with water and change the pH. A salt of a strong acid and strong base (NaCl) gives a neutral solution. Weak acid with strong base (CH₃COONa) gives a basic one. Strong acid with weak base (NH₄Cl) gives an acidic one. If both are weak, the pH depends on their Ka and Kb.","w":["Strong acid + strong base salt: neutral. Strong acid + weak base: acidic. Weak acid + strong base: basic.","For a weak acid + weak base salt compare Ka and Kb: Ka > Kb acidic, Ka < Kb basic, equal neutral (CH₃COONH₄)."]},"3.8":{"s":"A buffer resists big changes in pH when a little acid, base or water is added. An acidic buffer is a weak acid plus its salt (acetic acid with sodium acetate); a basic buffer is a weak base plus its salt. Henderson's equation: pH = pKa + log([salt]/[acid]). Blood is a buffered solution.","f":[{"x":"pH = pK_a + log_10([salt]/[acid])","n":"Henderson-Hasselbalch equation for an acidic buffer; pK_a = -log_10(K_a).","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 3.8, p.56"},{"x":"pOH = pK_b + log_10([salt]/[base])","n":"Basic buffer; then pH = 14 - pOH.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 3.8, p.56"}],"w":["Dilution does not change a buffer's pH: the ratio [salt]/[acid] stays the same."]},"3.9":{"s":"Take a saturated solution of a sparingly soluble salt. Multiply the ion concentrations, each raised to its coefficient. The result is constant: the solubility product Ksp. It links to solubility S; for AgCl, Ksp = S². A precipitate forms when the ionic product exceeds Ksp.","f":[{"x":"K_sp = [B^(y+)]^x*[A^(x-)]^y","n":"Solubility product of B_xA_y from saturated-solution concentrations.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 3.9, p.58"},{"x":"K_sp = x^x*y^y*S^(x + y)","n":"In terms of molar solubility S: AgBr S^2, PbI_2 4S^3, Al(OH)_3 27S^4.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 3.9, p.58"}],"w":["Precipitation happens only when the ionic product is GREATER than Ksp.","After mixing two solutions, halve (or rescale) each concentration before finding the ionic product."]},"3.10":{"s":"Adding an ion that is already in the equilibrium pushes it back and suppresses ionisation: the common ion effect. Adding NH₄Cl suppresses the ionisation of NH₄OH. It also lowers the solubility of a sparingly soluble salt. Salt analysis uses it to precipitate groups of cations separately.","w":["A common ion lowers the solubility of a sparingly soluble salt, but Ksp itself stays the same."]}};
